<header class="hero"><div class="shell"><a href="/guides/">← All guides</a><p class="eyebrow">Nashville market guide</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="dek">Builders are increasingly competing with both incentives and direct asking-price reductions. That changes the competitive set for resale sellers.</p><p class="byline">By Jason Kaczmarski · November 20, 2026 · 5 minute read</p></div></header>
<div class="shell body">
<p class="lead">If you are selling a resale home in Middle Tennessee, your competition may not be the house down the street. In builder-heavy areas, it may be a new home with a lower advertised price, subsidized financing, or both.</p>

<p>That matters because the new-construction market is under pressure. In November, the National Association of Home Builders reported that its Housing Market Index fell to <strong>25</strong>, the lowest level since April 2012. Its prospective-buyer-traffic index fell to <strong>16</strong>, the weakest reading in 15 years outside the early-pandemic disruption. <a href="https://www.nahb.org/news-and-economics/press-releases/2026/11/builder-sentiment-continues-to-fall-on-affordability-concerns">Source: NAHB</a>.</p>

<h2>Builders are using more than one lever</h2>

<p>NAHB reported that <strong>46% of builders reduced prices in November</strong>, the highest share in the post-Covid period. The average reported reduction was 6%. At the same time, <strong>68% of builders were using sales incentives</strong>, also a post-Covid high.</p>

<p>Those are national figures, not Nashville-only statistics. But the pattern is visible locally.</p>

<p>Dwellmark, which tracks asking-price changes directly from builder websites, recorded multiple Beazer Homes reductions around the Nashville metro in November. Recent examples included:</p>

<table><thead><tr><th>Community</th><th>Tracked asking-price change</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Parson's Valley, Thompson's Station</td><td>$871,990 → $795,990</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Mill at McFarlin, Nolensville</td><td>$813,990 → $739,990</td></tr>
<tr><td>Windtree, Mount Juliet</td><td>$779,990 → $709,990</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<p><small>These are dated changes to published asking prices tracked from Beazer Homes's own website—not appraised-value changes or guaranteed current offers. Builder pricing and incentives can change without notice. <a href="https://dwellmark.house/builders/beazer/nashville/price-cuts">Source: Dwellmark</a>.</small></p>

<p>As of November 20, Dwellmark was tracking <strong>1,105 new-construction homes from 18 builders across the Nashville metro</strong> and had recorded <strong>142 price-cut events in the prior seven days</strong>. Its coverage is a subset of the market, not the entire MLS, but it gives a useful real-time look at how builders are adjusting published prices. <a href="https://dwellmark.house/?metro=nashville">View the Nashville tracker</a>.</p>

<h2>Why this matters to resale sellers</h2>

<p>For much of the past year, builders could compete without visibly lowering prices. They could offer a mortgage-rate buydown, closing-cost assistance, appliance packages, or other incentives while protecting the published price of the home.</p>

<p>That still happens. But when builders also begin making direct asking-price cuts, the competition becomes harder for nearby resale sellers to ignore.</p>

<p>Imagine a buyer comparing a resale home with a brand-new home that has recently been reduced—and the builder is also offering financing assistance. The buyer is not simply comparing two list prices. They are comparing the entire cost of owning each home.</p>

<p>That can put pressure on resale homes even if the resale seller has no obvious new-construction comp in the immediate subdivision.</p>

<h2>Wilson and Rutherford counties deserve extra attention</h2>

<p>This issue matters most in parts of Middle Tennessee where buyers have a large menu of new construction. Mount Juliet, Lebanon, Smyrna, Murfreesboro, Antioch and other growth corridors give buyers more opportunities to compare an existing home with a builder's quick-move-in inventory.</p>

<p>That does not mean every resale listing needs to chase builder pricing. Established neighborhoods can offer advantages new construction does not: larger lots, mature trees, finished improvements, location, character, or a shorter commute.</p>

<p>But sellers should know what the nearby builders are offering before setting or defending a price.</p>

<h2>Do not compare list price alone</h2>

<p>A builder's advertised price is only one part of the transaction. Before deciding whether new construction is truly cheaper, compare:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Actual asking price.</strong> Has the builder recently reduced the home?</li>
<li><strong>Financing incentives.</strong> Is there a temporary or permanent rate buydown?</li>
<li><strong>Closing-cost assistance.</strong> How much is available, and does it require a preferred lender?</li>
<li><strong>Upgrade value.</strong> Are appliances, blinds, fencing or other items included?</li>
<li><strong>HOA and tax differences.</strong> New communities can have different recurring costs.</li>
<li><strong>Timing.</strong> Is the home complete, or does the buyer have to wait?</li>
</ul>

<p>That is why I think resale pricing should increasingly be based on <strong>the buyer's real alternatives</strong>, not just the most recent three closed resale comps.</p>

<h2>A price cut is not automatically a market collapse</h2>

<p>Builder discounting can look dramatic when viewed property by property. But a builder reducing a completed home by $40,000 or $60,000 does not mean every nearby resale property suddenly lost the same amount of value.</p>

<p>Builders carry inventory, construction loans, staffing costs and year-end financial targets. They may be willing to sacrifice margin on a specific completed home to free up capital.</p>

<p>The more important question is whether buyers begin to treat those discounted new homes as the new benchmark for value.</p>

<p>If builder reductions become broader, persist into 2027, or begin resetting base prices on future homes—not merely clearing isolated quick-move-in inventory—the impact on resale pricing would be more significant.</p>

<h2>What I would do before listing a home right now</h2>

<p>In builder-heavy areas, I would review three competitive sets:</p>

<ol>
<li>Recent resale closings.</li>
<li>Current resale listings and pendings.</li>
<li>Nearby new-construction inventory, including current incentives and price histories.</li>
</ol>

<p>The third category is increasingly important.</p>

<section class="callout"><p class="eyebrow">The takeaway</p><h2>Resale sellers are competing with the builder's entire offer—not just the builder's list price.</h2><p>As builder traffic weakens, more builders are cutting asking prices while continuing to use incentives. Sellers in new-construction-heavy areas should factor that into pricing and negotiation strategy.</p></section>

<p>If you are considering selling in Mount Juliet, Lebanon, Nashville, Smyrna, Murfreesboro or another Middle Tennessee market with significant new construction, I can help compare your home against both resale competition and nearby builder inventory before you decide how to price it.</p>

<p>You can also read my earlier guide on <a href="/guides/nashville-builder-mortgage-rate-incentives-october-2026/">builder mortgage-rate incentives</a> to see why the buyer's monthly payment can matter as much as the advertised price.</p>

<p><small>Market conditions, builder pricing and incentives can change quickly. The examples above are educational and should be independently verified before making a purchase, sale or financing decision.</small></p>
</div></article>
